{"answer":"Ireland’s green, white and orange tricolour symbolizes two traditions and the hope of peace between them: green represents the Gaelic tradition, orange represents the followers of William of Orange in Ireland, and white expresses the aspiration for peace between them. [S1]

Its historical importance is tied to Irish nationalism. The colours were used as a nationalist emblem as early as September 1830. Young Ireland leader Thomas Francis Meagher publicly unveiled the flag in Waterford on 7 March 1848; its design was inspired by the tricolours of France and Newfoundland. [S3]

The flag became popular through its use during the Easter Rising of 1916. Adopted as the Irish Free State’s flag in 1922, it continues as the state’s sole national flag and ensign. [S1]","abstain":false,"citations":["S1","S3"]}
